# kk: keep native 32-bit integer subgroup reductions
# https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/-/merge_requests/46470 state=merged
# ### What does this MR do and why?

<!--Describe in detail what your merge request does and why.-->

```
kk: keep native 32-bit integer subgroup reductions

KosmicKrisp lowers every subgroup reduction to a shuffle tree, although Metal has simd_sum, simd_product, simd_min, simd_max, simd_and, simd_or and simd_xor. This keeps 32-bit integer reductions over the whole subgroup (cluster size 0 or at least the subgroup size) native. Float reductions stay lowered because Metal's native ones fail the dEQP subgroup arithmetic tests; 8, 16 and 64-bit reductions and smaller clusters stay lowered too. The emitter now asserts that a reduction it emits has no smaller cluster.

In the dEQP integer add, and and max compute shaders each kernel goes from 30 shuffles to none. In Enshrouded (through Wine) the average frame rate goes from 30.2 to 30.5 FPS (+1.1% over three paired runs).

Testing (Apple M5 Pro, macOS 27.2): dEQP-VK.subgroups (10,030 cases, no case changes result; 112 integer reduce and 112 integer clustered reduce cases pass) and a 79,312 case list, with no regressions against main. Helper invocations in fragment shaders are not covered by any CTS case I found.
